{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>And he shall act</b> — This is Constantine, who was a great king over Babylon and Persia and Egypt and Africa, and he returned Babylon to his religion; likewise his associates returned the Persians and the Sabeans and Egypt and Africa to his religion, and he abandoned the religion of Aram. The sense of <b>and he shall exalt himself</b>: in his heart, and against HaShem, who is El of elohim, he shall speak wondrous things. And behold, the angel said of him, <b>and he shall prosper</b>. Now these things that were destined to happen in the days of Daniel have already passed, and now he begins to speak of the futures that remain. And know that Aram is northwest of the land of Israel, and Constantinople too is north. And at the time of the end, when the end of the king of the north of whom he said <b>and he shall prosper</b> shall come, until the wrath is finished, then the end of the Redeemer shall come, as will be explained. And behold, he did not mention when this shall be, only that this king — the sense being his son who went out and waged war with the king of Egypt and captured Egypt and went to that land, and then he shall be killed. And after days known in number from the day of his death, the Redeemer shall come to Israel."], :book :daniel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "I read 'והטעם בנו' as 'the sense being: his son,' taking בנו as son of the king; the syntax is compressed and the referent of the final narrative (who is killed, whose death triggers the redemption) is left unspecified by Ibn Ezra."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Daniel - Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/daniel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:21:24.748321490Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}